<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Nicaragua</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1838 CE · capital León</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Member state of the Central American Federation, crippled by the feud between liberal León and conservative Granada.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>. It adopted its constitution in 1826, with León as capital, but its towns had already fought each other in 1824 over who should hold power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rivalry between León, seat of liberal lawyers and officials, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, home of conservative merchants trading through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nicaragua">Lake Nicaragua</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-river-nicaragua">San Juan River</a>, produced repeated civil wars. Heads of state rarely lasted: Manuel Antonio de la Cerda was executed in 1828, and José Zepeda was murdered in 1837. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> ranching and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> sustained a small economy, while the British-allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A constituent assembly declared <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> independent of the federation on 30 April 1838, the first state to leave, creating the Republic of Nicaragua. León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> carried their feud into the new republic, and the coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> remained outside its control.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1824 CE – 1838 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.00%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1838 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>León</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuel Antonio de la Cerda, Juan Argüello</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estado de Nicaragua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1831&amp;focus=state-of-nicaragua" aria-label="State of Nicaragua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2294 1714 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Nicaragua lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2614 1970L2525 1889M2614 1970L2668 1949"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2525 1889h0M2668 1949h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2614 1970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2614 1970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks León. <a href="/atlas?year=1831&amp;focus=state-of-nicaragua">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
